The dry ashing process involved heating one gram of dry mushroom sample at 450 °C for 5 to 6 h in a muffle furnace. The resulting ash was dissolved in 2 ml of a di-acid mixture (HClO4 and HNO3 in a 3:10 ratio) and gently heated until transparent. The solution was then cooled, filtered into a 50 ml volumetric flask, and diluted to 50 ml with triple-distilled water. Each treatment was replicated three times and analyzed separately for phosphorus and potassium using specific protocols. Calcium estimation was performed using a Shimadzu spectrophotometer [28].

To calculate fiber content in shiitake mushrooms, a known quantity of fat-free sample was combined with 175 ml of distilled water and 25 ml of 10% H2SO4, refluxed for 30 min, filtered, and washed with hot distilled water. The residue was then refluxed with 175 ml of distilled water and 25 ml of 10% NaOH for 30 min, filtered through a pre-weighed Gooch crucible under vacuum, dried at 100 °C, and weighed. The residue was ashed at 550 to 560 °C for 3 h, and the loss in weight due to ashing was calculated as crude fiber, expressed as a percentage of the original sample weight [29].

The polysaccharide extraction process began with sieving fruit body powder through a 30-mesh sieve. 10 g of the sample was placed in a flask with 100 ml of pure water, and heated at 70 °C in a water bath with a reflux condensing tube for 30 min, filtered. The filtrate was collected and concentrated under reduced pressure. Anhydrous ethanol was added to the concentrated Liquid and centrifuged. The precipitate was washed with ethanol, ether, and acetone, then dried to obtain a crude polysaccharide. This crude polysaccharide was dissolved in hot water, extracted with ethyl acetate, and the process was repeated three times. The polyphenol water phase was treated with Sevag reagent, stirred, and centrifuged, and then the upper layer polysaccharide solution was diluted with distilled water. For analysis, a sulfuric acid-anthrone reagent was added to the polysaccharide solutions, heated, cooled, and the absorbance was measured at 620nm to calculate the polysaccharide content based on a glucose standard [30].

